{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The subject's single five-valued result domain: satisfied means the declared predicate holds on the exact basis; falsified means a declared counterexample defeats it; indeterminate means available admissible evidence cannot decide it; out_of_frame means a required material relation or capability is undeclared; invalid_basis means the subject or basis cannot support the evaluation. These are meanings described by the subject, not results assigned by this interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 801,
          "quote": "| `satisfied` | the declared predicate holds on the exact basis |\n| `falsified` | a declared counterexample defeats it |\n| `indeterminate` | the available admissible evidence cannot decide it |\n| `out_of_frame` | the evaluation requires an undeclared material relation or capability |\n| `invalid_basis` | the subject or basis cannot support the evaluation |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 797
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_result_algebra",
      "label": "Core result algebra"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A performed evaluation's recorded core value, evaluated predicate and original domain outcome. Result standing concerns what the result establishes about its predicate, separately from commitment standing. An evaluation not performed has no closed result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 816,
          "quote": "Recording. A result records its core value together with the predicate\n evaluated",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 811
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_result",
      "label": "Evaluation result and standing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A member, profile or adopting Product needs finer outcomes."
      ],
      "definition": "A domain-owned refinement names its question and mapping predicate and assigns every refined outcome exactly one core value. Further refinements may split a parent by cause while retaining that parent's core value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 865,
          "quote": "Refinements. A member, profile or adopting Product that needs finer\noutcomes defines a refinement. The refinement names the question it\nanswers and the predicate its mapping is for, and maps every refined value\nto exactly one core value.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 862
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_refinement",
      "label": "Result refinement"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Availability, assessment and acceptance are separate result domains with separate predicates: a step produced a candidate; the candidate meets declared criteria; the decision owner accepts it at its boundary.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 889,
          "quote": "Availability, assessment and acceptance are separate results, each with its\nown predicate: that a step produced a candidate; that the candidate meets\nits declared criteria; and that the\n[decision owner](ACTIVATION.md#roles-and-delegation) accepts it at its\nboundary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 885
        }
      ],
      "key": "candidate_result_domains",
      "label": "Availability, assessment and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source-defined operation combining results into one result for a composite predicate, including component claims, decisions over evaluations and outer contracts of composed steps. It preserves distinctions among failure, undecidability, non-performance and uncertain effects.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1014,
          "quote": "Conjunction combines results into one result for a composite predicate: a\nclaim made of component claims, a decision over several evaluations, or\nthe outer contract of composed steps. It is defined here once, over the\ncore.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1010
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_conjunction",
      "label": "Conjunction of results"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Three separate conjunction inputs: applicable returned closed results with predicates and domain outcomes; planned assessments lacking an applicable closed result; and required coverage addressed by no planned assessment, computed as required obligations R minus admitted planned coverage C.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1030,
          "quote": "1. the closed results returned, each a core value with its predicate and\n   domain outcome;\n2. the planned assessments that have no closed result applicable to the\n   composite: evaluations the composite requires that were not performed,\n   or whose result is absent, stale, bound to another subject or basis, or\n   incapable of establishing its claim;\n3. the required coverage that no planned assessment addresses: for a\n   required obligation set R and the coverage C that the planned\n   assessments admit, the uncovered coverage R minus C.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1022
        }
      ],
      "key": "conjunction_inputs",
      "label": "Returned results and two kinds of gap"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The member's or profile's declared decision rule specifying mandatory results, conflict and indeterminacy handling, required basis relations and coherent composite basis, decision owner, treatment of nonmandatory results, and possible veto or deferral.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1116,
          "quote": "The declared rule. A conjunction declares which results are mandatory; how\nconflict and indeterminacy are handled; which basis relations must agree,\nresults on different bases combining only under a declared coherent\ncomposite basis (§2, Basis); which decision authority owns the\nconjunction; how results that are not mandatory count; and whether any\nresult may veto or defer the decision.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FConjunction%20of%20results",
          "start_line": 1111
        }
      ],
      "key": "declared_conjunction_rule",
      "label": "Declared conjunction rule"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Family F3 traces result and combination rules to source commitments, instance premises, added choices, owners and pillars. It also records laws concerning imports, typed composition, conservation accounting, reference adequacy, latitude, shared machinery and categorical demonstrations. Its consumer lists describe applications of owner rules rather than additional ownership.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 951,
          "quote": "Instance premises, which the commitments do not supply:\n(i) an evaluation declares its predicate, subject and basis; (ii) results\nof several evaluations, steps or members are combined into one decision;\n(iii) members, profiles and adopting Products need outcomes finer than the\ncore.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 947
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1006,
          "quote": "Each consumer applies the rule and decides nothing the owning section does\nnot (P2, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FResults",
          "start_line": 1005
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_law_trace",
      "label": "Result foundation-to-law and consumer trace"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Applicable instance premises are construction with intake-bound basis and material assumptions Γ, declared case domain Ω, and a checker with declared observations and an admitted evaluation relation."
      ],
      "definition": "The subject's shared owner of preservation, assertion, provenance, quantified-assurance, evidence-reuse and formal-representation obligations. It connects construction to claims and supporting observations and evidence. The listed ACTIVATION, EVALUATION, ASSURANCE, RELEASE and SPEC_METHOD consumers apply these obligations without introducing second relations; the interpretation recording support kinds is a derived view.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1141,
          "quote": "They are the shared owner for\nthe preservation, assertion, provenance, quantified-assurance, reuse and\nformal-representation obligations in this corpus; ACTIVATION §1, §4, §5 and §7, EVALUATION §1 and §3,\nASSURANCE §3, §10, §11 and §14, RELEASE §8 and §9, SPEC_METHOD §4 and\nthe a_c interpretation apply them and state no second relation (P6). They are law\ncarried from the common assurance requirement. A tenant chooses its\nrepresentation and execution within these obligations. The notation\nstates proof obligations; writing an implication does not discharge it.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssurance%20relations",
          "start_line": 1134
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_relations",
      "label": "Assurance relations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Construction binds starting state A, required outcome Z, governing intake basis and material assumptions Γ."
      ],
      "definition": "Preservation across state or trace boundaries X_i, using declared transition relations F_i ⊆ X_i × X_(i+1) and properties P_i. Its universally quantified step obligation is: for all x and x', P_i(x) and F_i(x,x') imply P_(i+1)(x'). Z includes required effects and trace properties where material.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1184,
          "quote": "A construction binds its starting state A and required outcome Z under\n[ACTIVATION §1 Construction relation](ACTIVATION.md#construction-relation);\nthe governing basis bound at its intake and the material assumptions are\ntogether written Γ here. The\npredicate for Z includes required effects and trace properties where\nthese matter.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line": 1179
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1192,
          "quote": "For all x, x':  P_i(x) and F_i(x, x')  implies  P_(i+1)(x').",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FPreservation%20through%20construction",
          "start_line": 1192
        }
      ],
      "key": "construction_preservation",
      "label": "Preservation through construction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A source obligation r, Γ and declared case domain Ω have been selected."
      ],
      "definition": "Q_r = interpret(r, Γ, Ω), the required predicate retaining the source obligation's scope, conditions, exceptions and permitted alternatives.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1236,
          "quote": "For a selected source obligation r, let `Q_r = interpret(r, Γ, Ω)` be its\nrequired predicate over the declared domain Ω of cases. The interpretation\nretains the obligation's scope, conditions, exceptions and permitted\nalternatives.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line": 1232
        }
      ],
      "key": "interpreted_obligation",
      "label": "Interpreted source obligation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An assertion targets q_t and identifies whether it is the full obligation or a narrower claim. A stated conjunction q_1 and ... and q_n can support a sufficiency claim, subject to its own coverage and compatibility argument.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1244,
          "quote": "Q_r implies q_t                   : q_t is a necessary condition of Q_r.\nq_t implies Q_r                   : establishing q_t is sufficient for Q_r.\n(q_1 and ... and q_n) implies Q_r : the stated conjunction is sufficient.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line": 1242
        }
      ],
      "key": "assertion_predicate",
      "label": "Assertion predicate and sufficient assertion conjunction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A checker observes π: Ω → O and admits conclusions through T ⊆ O × R, where R is the core result algebra. Deterministic checkers are functional special cases; human or probabilistic checkers can admit multiple conclusions for the same observations. sat and fal abbreviate judgments about q_t, not execution states.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1257,
          "quote": "Let `π: Ω → O` describe the observations available to a checker and\n`T ⊆ O × R` its admitted evaluation relation, where R is the core result\nalgebra of Results above. A deterministic checker is a functional special\ncase. A human or probabilistic checker can admit different conclusions on\nthe same observations; a universal soundness claim covers all admitted\nconclusions, not a favourable selected run.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FAssertions%20and%20observation",
          "start_line": 1252
        }
      ],
      "key": "observation_checker",
      "label": "Observation and admitted evaluation relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Assertion provenance records the owning obligation, interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared dependencies of an expected result or acceptance predicate. Execution provenance records actual checker, exact subject, applicable environment, inputs, observations and result. Assessment authentication separately binds result to producer, subject, basis and evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1323,
          "quote": "An expected result or acceptance predicate has assertion provenance: the\nowning obligation, its interpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared\ndependencies, which give it meaning. An execution has execution\nprovenance: the actual checker, exact subject, applicable environment,\ninputs, observations and result. Both are needed for the claims they\nsupport; execution records alone cannot establish that the expected\nanswer follows from the requirement. Authentication of a returned\nassessment, which binds a result to its producer, subject, basis and\nevidence ([EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment)), is a\nthird thing and establishes neither provenance (P6, P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1314
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_provenance",
      "label": "Assertion provenance, execution provenance and authentication"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Compared constructions or assertions have independent justification within their stated scope."
      ],
      "definition": "Mutual assurance compares such constructions or assertions. Parallel paths may derive checks from a requirement or its refinement and apply them to a realisation; acceptance and build-level tests are examples, not prescribed stage names or classes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1312,
          "quote": "Acceptance tests and build-level tests can\ninstantiate these paths\n([ASSURANCE §14](ASSURANCE.md#14-design-to-module-evidence-route)); the\nroles identify the selected sources and subject in this example and\nprescribe no stage names or test classes (P1, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1308
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1336,
          "quote": "Mutual assurance compares independently justified constructions or\nassertions within their stated scope.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FParallel%20derivation%20and%20mutual%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1334
        }
      ],
      "key": "mutual_assurance",
      "label": "Parallel derivation and mutual assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Quantified assurance is claimed."
      ],
      "definition": "A model of a stated population or uncertainty, prior information, likelihoods, shared causes and calibration limits. For violation V and evidence E it states the conditional posterior P(V|E,Γ); for checks it distinguishes the joint probability of missing V from the chance of at least one detection and from whole-product correctness.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1370,
          "quote": "Where quantified assurance is claimed, its model states the population or\nuncertainty being modelled, prior information, likelihoods, shared causes\nand calibration limits. For a specified violation event V and evidence\nevent E:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FQuantified%20assurance",
          "start_line": 1367
        }
      ],
      "key": "quantified_assurance",
      "label": "Risk model and quantified assurance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Change impact and evidence reuse distinguish assertion-justification dependencies from dependencies affecting evidence behaviour, conclusion or applicability. Δ includes changed material facts, dependencies and newly discovered invalid assumptions. I(Δ) conservatively covers changed facts and affected dependents; D*(t) covers transitive dependencies material to check t's assertion, execution and evidence use. Candidate selection S_Δ contains checks whose D*(t) intersects I(Δ).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1422,
          "quote": "Let Δ be a change to material facts or dependencies, including a newly\ndiscovered invalid assumption. Let `I(Δ)`, the impact cover, conservatively cover the changed\nfacts and their affected dependents. For each check t, let `D*(t)` cover\nthe transitive dependencies material to its assertion, execution and use\nof evidence. A candidate selection is:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1418
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1425,
          "quote": "S_Δ = { t : D*(t) intersects I(Δ) }.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1425
        }
      ],
      "key": "evidence_reuse",
      "label": "Change impact, reassessment and evidence reuse"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The candidate's formal interpretation represents material obligations and dependencies while distinguishing five kinds of support: stated formula, discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked structure, externally assessed predicate and execution evidence. Source semantics, formal fidelity and actual use keep separate evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1504,
          "quote": "It distinguishes five kinds of support for a statement: a\nstated formula, a discharged mathematical argument, mechanically checked\nstructure, an externally assessed predicate, and evidence from execution.\nAn external predicate retains the source, scope and evidence required to\nestablish it; an arbitrary truth label does not discharge the obligation.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F4.%20Algebra%2FFormal%20representation",
          "start_line": 1500
        }
      ],
      "key": "formal_representation",
      "label": "Formal representation and support kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Claims carried for falsification, expressly not law: categorical equivalence of 𝒜 and ℐ under some reformulation; universal algorithmic capture of meaning; statistical Markov boundary with candidate status; and traversal composition laws of any runtime.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1536,
          "quote": "Not law; carried for falsification: categorical equivalence of 𝒜 and ℐ under\nsome reformulation; universal algorithmic capture of meaning; the statistical\nMarkov boundary (candidate status under\n[WORLD_MODEL §4](WORLD_MODEL.md#epistemic-status); provenance\n[WORLD_MODEL_METHOD](stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#epistemic-status-of-the-construct));\ntraversal composition laws of any runtime.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F5.%20Hypotheses",
          "start_line": 1531
        }
      ],
      "key": "falsifiable_hypotheses",
      "label": "Hypotheses without law standing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The subject's predecessor-clause disposition register, rather than an independently verified conservation result. It reports AC position/provenance/scope in AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §1; signature and five typed records in §§2–3; AC-001 through AC-014 in §4, with generic object, result, latitude, reference and conservation meanings located in FOUNDATIONS; traversals and AC-015/016 in §5; interpretation and AC-017/018/019 in §6; carrier boundaries and AC-020 in §7; exact identity in §8 and its record as a release artefact; application/import/exclusion/conformance in §§9–10. RF definitions are routed to FOUNDATIONS §2; RF laws, functions, coverage, declarations and closure to REFERENCE_FRAME, with results and conjunction defined once here; engagement and qualification to ACTIVATION and EVALUATION. UP-001/004/008/022 are consumed by EVALUATION, UP-004 also restated in IDENTITY; UP-013 to SPEC_METHOD §3, UP-014 to ACTIVATION §6 and SPEC_METHOD §8, UP-005 to DESIGN_MODULE §6 and ACTIVATION §6; UP-015 to RELEASE §9 and EVALUATION §5; UP-016/018/019/023 to DESIGN_MODULE §§5/7 with general admission, intake and sharing rules elsewhere; UP-017/021 to DESIGN_MODULE §5 and ACTIVATION §4; UP-020 to ACTIVATION §§1/3/5/7. Release identities, scoped identity, work boundary and product taxonomy are routed to their named FOUNDATIONS, RELEASE, IDENTITY and SPEC_METHOD owners. Semantic imports are owned by FOUNDATIONS §2 and refined between bounded contexts by PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5; ambiguity is routed to SPEC_METHOD §5 and ACTIVATION §§3/4. ODD, DESIGN_MODULE, UX and WORLD_MODEL profile law remains in those members, indexed by PROFILES, without import into tier 1.",
      "exceptions": [
        "The AC Fundamental Laws heading is reported inapplicable because it has no claim text, 20 bytes.",
        "The RF definitions heading is reported inapplicable because it has no claim text, 15 bytes; its children are disposed individually."
      ],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1543,
          "quote": "| AC Position, Derivation Provenance, Scope | conserved; restated in AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §1 |\n| AC Core Signature, Typed Records and its five records | conserved; restated in AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §2 and §3; Semantic Object and Residual are related to the §2 objects Object and Residual, the definitions stay here |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F6.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1542
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1573,
          "quote": "| ODD, DESIGN_MODULE, UX, WORLD_MODEL profile law | conserved; restated in full in the ODD, DESIGN_MODULE, UX and WORLD_MODEL members, which dispose their rows and are indexed in PROFILES §2 to §5; nothing imported into tier 1. P0 names among its sources the refinement of ACTIVATION §4, which generalises ODD's graph functions |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F6.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1573
        }
      ],
      "key": "source_disposition_register",
      "label": "Authored predecessor dispositions and owner routes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "GLOSSARY is a non-deciding locator guide from indexed spellings to this corpus's concept-owning clauses and bounded contexts. It realises the glossary named by PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 without adding law. Routes written rc.2 #anchor point into stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/GLOSSARY_GUIDE.md as provenance only, not an application dependency. Clauses name their requiring principle P0–P6.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 10,
